WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Secure C sharp development works best as a repeatable workflow.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:12.200
It starts in design, where teams identify trust boundaries, sensitive data, privileged operations, dependencies, and deployment assumptions.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:22.140
It continues through implementation, where validation, authorization, error handling, data protection, and safe APIs become normal engineering tasks.

4
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.420
Code review should look for clear ownership of security decisions.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.840
Where is input validated?

6
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.940
Where is authorization enforced?

7
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.880
Where are secrets stored?

8
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.820
What data is logged?

9
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.920
What dependencies changed?

10
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.900
What happens when an operation fails?

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.040
These questions are easier to answer when teams use consistent patterns rather than one-off fixes.

12
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.640
Testing and deployment connect the workflow.

13
00:00:49.740 --> 00:01:01.580
Unit tests, integration tests, security-focused test cases, configuration review, dependency review, release approvals, and monitoring all reinforce each other.

14
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.280
No single control is enough, but together they make the application more predictable and easier to improve.

15
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:30.440
The practical takeaway is to build small habits into everyday work: validate at the server, encode output correctly, separate authentication from authorization, check object-level access, protect secrets, avoid custom cryptography, use parameterized data access, handle files carefully, log safely, review dependencies, and treat deployment as part of security.

16
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.880
Secure C sharp development is continuous improvement.

17
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.860
The goal is not a perfect application on the first pass.

18
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:47.320
The goal is a codebase whose assumptions are visible, whose controls are repeatable, and whose team can keep improving as the application, platform, and threat environment change.
